{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-15202",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "From the fundamental theorem of calculus, we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =f(4)-f(-1) \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =f(4)-2\\pi  \\\\ f(4) & ={\\int }_{-4}^{1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x+2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that the integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis.\n\nThe graph consists of $2$ parts: two line segments for $x\\in [-1,3]$ and the arc of a quarter-circle for $x\\in [3,4].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe first integral ${A}_{1}$ represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,3].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second integral ${A}_{2}$ represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,4].$ The corresponding region is a quarter-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi \\cdot (1)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of the original integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =5+\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the value of the integral, we can find $f(4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & ={\\int }_{-4}^{1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x+2\\pi  \\\\  & =5+\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2\\pi  \\\\  & =5+\\frac{9\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
